<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    FR-06 positional focus marker — leading-edge vertical bar
    ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

    A 3dp bar pinned to the LEADING edge (not the left edge, so it mirrors
    correctly in RTL), spanning the full height of whatever it is drawn into.

    WHY IT IS A LAYER-LIST RATHER THAN A BARE <shape>

    This drawable has two consumers with different sizing needs:

      1. As a nested item inside an existing focus selector's layer-list, where
         the parent already controls placement.
      2. As a View.foreground on the ~45 TV cards that paint focus
         programmatically — there it must size ITSELF, because a foreground is
         stretched to the view's bounds and a bare <shape> with only a width
         would either fill the whole card or collapse to nothing.

    Wrapping the bar in a layer-list with an explicit width and start gravity
    makes it behave correctly in both: it fills the bounds it is given, and the
    bar inside sits at the leading edge at its own width, full height.

    WHY THE COLOUR IS AN ATTRIBUTE AND THE WIDTH IS NOT

    `?attr/ufmFocusMarkerColor` is a COLOUR reference — a form this codebase
    exercises in ~31 places across res/drawable and res/color. The width is a
    literal on purpose: a `format="dimension"` attribute read through
    `android:width="?attr/…"` is exercised NOWHERE in this project, and an
    unproven mechanism does not belong at the root of a 44-file conversion.
    Marker prominence therefore scales with strength by CONTRAST, not by width.

    ⚠ THE ONE NOVEL MECHANISM IN THIS FEATURE — CONFIRM ON DEVICE

    Measured across the whole repo (res/, not just res/drawable):

      • `android:width` + `android:gravity` on a layer-list <item> — ZERO
        occurrences. This file is the first.
      • A drawable referenced as a LAYER inside a layer-list
        (<item android:drawable="@drawable/…"/> within a <layer-list>) — ZERO
        occurrences. `android:drawable` appears only inside <selector> items.

    Both are standard framework behaviour (item width/height/gravity on
    LayerDrawable landed in API 23; minSdk here is 26), so they are expected to
    work — but "expected to work" is exactly the standard this refactor has
    refused everywhere else, so it is recorded rather than glossed.

    WHY THE PROVEN-FORMS-ONLY ALTERNATIVE WAS REJECTED: a fixed-width bar is not
    expressible with layer-list insets alone (that needs right = -(width-3dp),
    and width is unknown at design time). The only inset-only construction that
    yields a narrow leading cue is a layer that PROTRUDES past the element's
    left edge and is then covered by the fill — and on edge-to-edge TV rows that
    protrusion is a candidate for parent clipping, which would silently defeat
    FR-06 for exactly the rows that need it most. A standard framework feature
    is the better trade against a fragile workaround.

    IF IT IS WRONG, IT FAILS LOUDLY, NOT SILENTLY: without item width/gravity the
    bar stretches to fill the item's bounds, so with the mode ON the whole
    element turns the marker colour. That is unmissable in the first minute of
    device testing — check this marker FIRST (see tasks.md T011).

    OFF STATE

    When Colorblind Mode is Off, ufmFocusMarkerColor resolves to
    @android:color/transparent, so the bar occupies its 3dp inside the element's
    existing padding and draws nothing. That is why there is no separate
    "marker_none" drawable and no branching anywhere — and because the bar sits
    inside existing padding, it cannot clip content or reflow a row (NFR-02).
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item
        android:width="3dp"
        android:gravity="start|center_vertical">
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="?attr/ufmFocusMarkerColor" />
        </shape>
    </item>
</layer-list>
